title = "Natural Language Processing and Artificial Intelligence-Assisted Document Analysis in Science Education: The Case of Twenty Thousand Leagues under the Sea"
pages = [
[
("h","Purpose and rationale"),
("p","A science educator looking for a passage about pressure faces two decisions. A search can locate the word, but the educator must still decide whether the passage explains a physical relationship. A further reading is needed to decide what a student could do with that explanation. This study proposes a procedure for making both decisions traceable in qualitative document analysis. It illustrates the procedure with the English text of Jules Verne’s Twenty Thousand Leagues under the Sea and selected learning outcomes from Türkiye’s science curriculum. In P419, for instance, the narrator reports the thickness of a glass plate and the pressure it withstood. A teacher can ask which quantities were measured and how those measurements support the narrator’s claim."),
("p","Document analysis interprets evidence in the setting in which it appears (Bowen, 2009). Computational text analysis adds a means of finding patterns that warrant close reading (Nelson, 2020). Here the science categories were specified in advance. The methodological contribution is to connect each retrieved passage to its original wording, the reason for its proposed code, and the source of the decision. A second researcher can locate the same passage, apply the same criteria, and identify the point at which a different interpretation arose."),
("h","Preparing and searching the text"),
("p","The source was the English text of Project Gutenberg eBook 164 (Verne, 1994). The project notices and table of contents were removed, leaving 1,680 content paragraphs in their original order. Each retained paragraph has a stable identifier. Python code generated from Turkish instructions to ChatGPT was run in Google Colab. On a separate copy, regular expressions extracted lowercase alphabetic tokens. Tokens shorter than three letters and English stop words were removed, and NLTK’s PorterStemmer produced word stems. The original wording remained available for quotation and coding."),
("p","The paragraph search applied English keyword patterns to the original text. The first four thematic groups retrieved 622 paragraphs. A supplementary search added 37, yielding 659 unique candidates (39.23%). Natural environment and interactions accounted for 419 candidate paragraphs, living organisms and biodiversity for 222, and science and technology for 160. A passage could match several groups. The 1,021 paragraphs without a match remained eligible for contextual reading. Retaining these records allowed the researcher to examine scientific observations expressed in vocabulary the search had missed. Figure 1 summarizes what is retained at each step."),
],
[
("h","Making and recording coding decisions"),
("p","The coding unit was one paragraph. Code 0 applied when a term served an everyday, figurative, or purely narrative function. Code 1 indicated a scientific entity or process mentioned without a concrete observation or explanation. Code 2 required evidence such as a measurement, a classification, a description of a mechanism, or an explicit relationship between phenomena. For example, a character asking whether another person fears sharks received code 0 (P932). A passage referring to clouds and wind without explaining them received code 1 (P1201). A description of how a pearl forms and what it contains received code 2 (P921). These decisions can be checked against the paragraphs themselves."),
("p","For the pilot, six passages were selected from each of the five candidate groups and 20 from the paragraphs without a keyword match. The selection used the fixed random seed 20261015. The researcher first read the 50 passages without seeing the automated labels. A separate contextual AI suggestion was then available for comparison, and the researcher retained or revised the initial decision. The record keeps the initial human code, the contextual suggestion, the rule-based pre-code, and the final reviewed code in separate fields."),
("p","The rule-based pre-code and the final reviewed code coincided in 29 of 50 cases (58%). The disagreements show where reviewing the wording changes the interpretation. Of 20 paragraphs provisionally marked as indirect, nine contained direct evidence and six received code 0 after review. The no-match control group also yielded six direct and one indirect example. These counts describe decisions in a deliberately mixed pilot. One researcher made the human decisions, so independent agreement between human coders remains unmeasured."),
("h","What the corpus labels represent"),
("p","ChatGPT separately proposed a code, theme, and short rationale for 60 priority paragraphs. The combined preliminary distribution reported here draws on 50 researcher-reviewed pilot records, 60 AI contextual records, and 1,570 rule-based records. It contains 162 paragraphs labeled direct (9.64%), 474 indirect (28.21%), and 1,044 without science content (62.14%). The origin of each label is retained so that a reader can identify which passages received researcher review. A later automated pass is archived separately. These figures describe the specified coding stage and can be recomputed from its paragraph records."),
],
[
("h","From a passage to a curriculum decision"),
("p","The curriculum analysis distinguishes a topic from the action expected of a learner. In the 2024 Türkiye Century Maarif Model Science Curriculum, a passage may mention light while providing little evidence for an outcome that asks students to explain its straight-line propagation through observation (Ministry of National Education, 2024). The workflow therefore pairs the original paragraph with the wording of the outcome and records a rationale for a direct conceptual link (2), a partial link (1), or an unsupported link (0). Unscored pairs remain identifiable."),
("p","An exploratory unit-level search produced 976 candidate links in 437 paragraphs across a curriculum inventory of 44 units and 182 outcomes. A focused search identified 86 paragraph–outcome pairs for four selected outcomes in Grades 5, 6, and 8. Of the 62 pairs examined by AI in 60 paragraphs, seven received a direct rating, 27 a partial rating, and 28 an unsupported rating. The other 24 pairs were unscored. The selected teaching examples covered 19 content groups, including buoyancy, density, biodiversity, and electrical energy. All seven direct ratings concerned transformations of electrical energy. For example, the description of electricity supplying heat, light, and motion on the Nautilus gives a teacher specific transformations students could classify. These are proposed uses of selected passages. Their classroom effects were outside the scope of the study."),
("h","Reapplying the procedure"),
("p","To apply the method to another text, researchers can define what counts as scientific evidence, document the edition and paragraph boundaries, adapt the thematic dictionary, and preserve a sample of retrieval misses. The accompanying materials include the original preprocessing notebook, the four Turkish instructions documented in the manuscript with English translations, paragraph-level records, and a script that reproduces the counts. They also include a runnable example for a new search. The complete historical search dictionary and the exact ChatGPT model snapshot could not be established from the records. New applications should preserve the dictionary version, prompts, model identifier, and responses alongside the researcher’s decisions."),
("p","This case demonstrates how another educator can follow a passage from retrieval to an instructional decision and revisit the evidence at each step. Its empirical scope is one English edition, one researcher, and a limited set of curriculum outcomes. Independent coding of a larger sample would test the stability of the judgments. Comparing recorded analysis times would address a different question about workload. The present contribution is the documented procedure and the opportunity it gives readers to inspect the evidence and apply the same decision rules to a new text."),
],
]
